---
/**
 * /privacy — two parts that never mix.
 *
 * Part 1's subject is this website. Part 2's subject is the product. They are
 * kept in separate sections with separate headings because the single most
 * likely error on a page like this is a sentence that starts about one and
 * finishes about the other — "we don't collect anything" is true of the site's
 * code and true of the product for different reasons, and a reader who cannot
 * tell which is being claimed has been given nothing.
 *
 * ── THE ONE BOUNDARY THAT IS EASY TO GET WRONG ────────────────────────────
 *
 * PRIVACY.md says "The extension does not make any network requests. It works
 * fully offline." The subject of that sentence is the Chrome extension, not
 * VibeGuard. Widening it to the product would be a stronger claim than the
 * source document makes, and it would be false in a way a reader can catch: the
 * GitHub Action uploads SARIF to code scanning when the workflow asks it to.
 * What can be said about the whole product is the README's own line — VibeGuard
 * never sends your code anywhere — plus "analysis runs locally", both of which
 * survive the CI path existing.
 *
 * The two unflattering facts (reports carry the matched line; the Chrome
 * history keeps 200 raw characters per entry) are in the summary on purpose. A
 * summary that is kinder to the product than the document it summarises is not
 * a summary. They are also the two facts a reader is most likely to discover on
 * their own, and discovering them here costs nothing while discovering them
 * elsewhere costs the rest of the page its credibility.
 *
 * ── THE DATE IS READ, NOT TYPED ───────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * PRIVACY.md's own change policy is "commit the new version and update the Last
 * updated line". This page reads that line out of the file at build time, so a
 * visitor can see at a glance which revision this summary was made from and go
 * and check it. A typed date would be correct exactly until the next edit.
 */
import ProductLayout from '../layouts/ProductLayout.astro';

/*
 * ?raw is resolved by the bundler against this file's own location, so the path
 * does not depend on which directory the build was started from. Reading the
 * repository's PRIVACY.md directly also means this page has no second source of
 * truth to fall out of step with — there is no exported copy in between.
 */
import privacyMd from '../../../PRIVACY.md?raw';

const REPO = 'https://github.com/YUTAKONDO1205/VibeGuard';
const PRIVACY_MD_URL = `${REPO}/blob/main/PRIVACY.md`;
const ISSUES_URL = `${REPO}/issues`;
const NO_NETWORK_WORKFLOW_URL = `${REPO}/blob/main/.github/workflows/no-network-assert.yml`;

const lastUpdated = /^Last updated:\s*(\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2})\s*$/m.exec(privacyMd)?.[1];
if (!lastUpdated) {
  throw new Error(
    'Could not read the "Last updated:" line from PRIVACY.md. The summary on /privacy is dated from ' +
      'that line, and an undated summary gives a reader no way to tell whether it is current.',
  );
}

/**
 * The complete list of what the redirect counter does not keep. It is written
 * out item by item rather than summarised as "nothing identifying", because
 * "nothing identifying" is a judgement and this is a list — a reader can check
 * a list against the Worker source and cannot check a judgement against
 * anything.
 */
const NOT_STORED: readonly string[] = [
  'Your IP address — not raw, not hashed, not truncated',
  'Your user agent',
  'The referring page',
  'Your Accept-Language',
  'Cookies — none are set and none are read',
  'Any visitor identifier or session identifier, random ones included',
  'Country, region, or network operator',
];

/*
 * ── NOTES ON THE WORDING BELOW ───────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * These were HTML comments in the template until it turned out Astro emits
 * those into the built page. They explain why particular sentences are
 * phrased as they are; that reasoning belongs to whoever edits this file,
 * not to every visitor with a View Source menu.
 *
 * The tense here is load-bearing and was wrong.
 * This said the worker "increments a counter", present tense, as a
 * statement of what happens today. It does not: the analytics binding in
 * site/wrangler.jsonc is commented out pending confirmation that the
 * storage it needs is available on this account, and worker/index.ts
 * returns early when the binding is absent. So today the redirect
 * redirects and nothing is written at all.
 * Overstating what is collected is the harmless direction to be wrong in,
 * which is exactly why it is worth fixing rather than shrugging at: a
 * privacy page that is casually inaccurate about the safe cases gives a
 * reader no reason to trust it about the unsafe ones. The ceiling is
 * stated as a ceiling, and what is true right now is stated separately.
 */
---

<ProductLayout
  title="Privacy — VibeGuard"
  description="What this website stores (three fields, and the list of what it does not), and a summary of the product's privacy policy."
>
  <section class="vg-band vg-band--base" aria-labelledby="privacy-h">
    <div class="vg-band__inner vg-privacy">
      <h1 class="vg-h1" id="privacy-h">Privacy</h1>
      <p class="vg-privacy__lead">
        Two separate subjects, kept apart: this website, and the VibeGuard software. What is true of one is not
        automatically true of the other.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="vg-band vg-band--tint" aria-labelledby="site-h">
    <div class="vg-band__inner vg-privacy">
      <h2 class="vg-h2" id="site-h">
        <span class="vg-h2__deco" aria-hidden="true">//</span>Part 1 — This website
      </h2>

      <p>
        This site loads no third-party resources. No analytics, no tag manager, no error collector, no fonts or
        scripts or images from another host. Nothing runs in your browser: the pages are static HTML and CSS, and
        the Content-Security-Policy served with them says <code>script-src 'none'</code>, so a script could not run
        even if one were added by mistake. You can check that yourself with a single <code>curl -I</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        No cookies are set and none are read. Nothing is written to local storage, session storage, or IndexedDB.
        There are no forms anywhere on this site, so there is nothing to submit.
      </p>

      <h3 class="vg-privacy__h3">At most one thing is counted</h3>
      <p>
        When you follow a link to a distribution channel, the redirect is served by a small worker. The most that
        worker will ever record is a counter, and a record has exactly three fields:
      </p>
      <ol class="vg-privacy__fields">
        <li>which channel the link pointed at, from a fixed list of names</li>
        <li>the date in UTC</li>
        <li>an integer — how many times that day</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        That is the whole record. It is stated first rather than last because a page that mentions counting only
        after three paragraphs of denial reads as though the counting needed hiding, and three fields do not.
      </p>
      <p>
        As it stands the counter is not switched on: the storage it would write to has not been configured, and the
        worker skips the count when it is absent. So at the moment the redirect only redirects. This paragraph
        describes the ceiling either way — turning it on cannot add a fourth field without changing the code, this
        page, and the same commit.
      </p>

      <h3 class="vg-privacy__h3">What is not stored</h3>
      <ul class="vg-privacy__not">
        {NOT_STORED.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <p>
        The redirect worker does not read the request's headers or its body, so there is nothing to discard: the
        values above are not collected and then dropped, they are never looked at.
      </p>

      <h3 class="vg-privacy__h3">What the count cannot tell anyone</h3>
      <p>
        Crawlers and link prefetchers increment the counter like anyone else, and the number is not corrected for
        them — correcting it would mean inspecting the user agent, which is on the list above. Repeated clicks by
        one person are indistinguishable from clicks by several people, because nothing here distinguishes people.
        The number is a count of clicks. It is not a count of visitors, and it is not treated as one.
      </p>

      <h3 class="vg-privacy__h3">The limit of this statement</h3>
      <p>
        Everything above describes the code of this site. Hosting providers keep their own records at their own
        layer, and what those contain is not something this page has verified, so it is not claimed here either
        way.
      </p>

      <p class="vg-privacy__contact">
        Questions about any of this: <a href={ISSUES_URL}>{ISSUES_URL}</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="vg-band vg-band--base" aria-labelledby="product-h">
    <div class="vg-band__inner vg-privacy">
      <h2 class="vg-h2" id="product-h">
        <span class="vg-h2__deco" aria-hidden="true">//</span>Part 2 — The VibeGuard software
      </h2>
      <p class="vg-privacy__lead">
        A summary of <a href={PRIVACY_MD_URL}>PRIVACY.md</a>, with the subject of each statement named. The point of
        a summary is to show you that there is a document worth reading, not to save you from reading it.
      </p>

      <dl class="vg-privacy__summary">
        <dt>VibeGuard, the product</dt>
        <dd>
          VibeGuard does not collect, transmit, store on our servers, sell, or share any data about you, your
          browsing, or the source code you analyze. VibeGuard never sends your code anywhere.
        </dd>

        <dt>All four channels</dt>
        <dd>
          All security analysis runs locally — in your browser with the Chrome extension, or on your machine with
          the CLI and the GitHub Action.
        </dd>

        <dt>The Chrome extension only</dt>
        <dd>
          The extension does not make any network requests. It works fully offline. This sentence is about the
          extension; it is not a statement about the CLI or the Action, and the summary does not widen it into one.
        </dd>

        <dt>Reports you ask for</dt>
        <dd>
          A finding carries the matched line and the matched text. When you ask the CLI or the GitHub Action to
          write a report — with <code>--out</code>, or a format of <code>json</code>, <code>sarif</code> or{' '}
          <code>markdown</code> — those fields go into the file. If you then publish it, for example by uploading
          SARIF to GitHub code scanning, whatever the finding matched travels with it. A scan report is as
          sensitive as the code it describes.
        </dd>

        <dt>The Chrome extension, on your own device</dt>
        <dd>
          The side panel keeps a scan history in <code>chrome.storage.local</code>, which survives restarting the
          browser. Each entry holds the first 200 characters of what you scanned, stored verbatim — for a pasted
          snippet, that is your raw code, and a credential inside those characters is kept as written. At most 50
          entries are kept. The <b>Clear</b> button in the side panel's History section deletes them, and removing
          the extension does too.
        </dd>

        <dt>Third parties</dt>
        <dd>None. No analytics, no telemetry, no remote logging, no ad networks.</dd>
      </dl>

      <p>
        The no-network claim is machine-checked on every pull request, on every push to <code>main</code>, and again
        every Monday: <a href={NO_NETWORK_WORKFLOW_URL}>.github/workflows/no-network-assert.yml</a>. The workflow
        states its own scope, which is worth reading before treating it as covering more than it does.
      </p>

      <p class="vg-privacy__src">
        Summarised from <a href={PRIVACY_MD_URL}>PRIVACY.md</a>, last updated {lastUpdated}. Where this summary and
        that file disagree, the file is right.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</ProductLayout>
